    "eventMetadata": {
      "context_description": "Lula da Silva holds a commanding position in the 2026 Brazilian presidential race as the incumbent seeking a fourth term, bolstered by recent campaign emphasis on job creation and targeted economic measures such as debt relief and fuel subsidies. Flávio Bolsonaro, the leading right-wing contender endorsed by his ineligible father Jair, trails in first-round polling but narrows the gap in simulated runoffs amid voter concerns over crime and economic issues. Other candidates including Renan Santos and Ronaldo Caiado register minimal support. Recent Quaest surveys show Lula's runoff edge shrinking to statistical ties in some scenarios, reflecting tightening competition ahead of the October first round and potential second-round dynamics. Trader consensus on these probabilities aligns with polling trends and the structural advantages of incumbency in Brazil's electoral system.",
